# The Wythoff construction, the recipe that turns a reflection group plus a CHOICE OF MIRRORS into a uniform polytope,
# modeled concretely and proved by the kernel. The input is a Coxeter group, given by its Coxeter-Dynkin diagram, and a
# RING PATTERN: a subset of the diagram's nodes marked active (drawn as a ringed node). The output is a uniform polytope.
# The recipe is a single SEED POINT placed in the fundamental chamber, off every UNRINGED mirror and lying ON every
# RINGED mirror. The full vertex set is the ORBIT of that seed point under the whole group, the seed reflected by all the
# mirrors.
#
# The ring pattern is what makes a single group give many figures.
#   An UNRINGED node is an INACTIVE mirror. The seed lies on it, so reflecting in it FIXES the seed.
#   A RINGED node is an ACTIVE mirror. The seed lies off it, so reflecting in it MOVES the seed.
# So only the active mirrors actually move the seed, and the figure is the orbit under that motion. For the rank-two
# group [p] (the dihedral symmetry of a p-gon, two mirrors at angle pi/p) the ring choices give the whole family.
#   Ring ONE node       -> the regular p-gon            (a REGULAR figure: one ringed end node)
#   Ring BOTH nodes      -> the truncated p-gon, a 2p-gon (a TRUNCATED figure: every node ringed)
#   Ring the middle only -> the rectified figure          (the rectification, the alternate ringing)
# The all-unringed pattern is degenerate: no mirror is active, the seed never moves, and the orbit is a single point.
#
# This file models the discrete combinatorial core for [p]. It carries a ring state per node, a marking over a rank-two
# diagram, an `is-active` test, a `reflect-if-active` reflection that acts only when its node is ringed, and a vertex
# count read off the ring pattern. The kernel proves: an inactive reflection leaves the seed unchanged, an active one
# changes it, the [3] ring patterns give a triangle (three vertices) and a hexagon (six vertices), the rectified middle
# gives the rectified count, two patterns of the same group give different counts (the rectification / truncation
# relationship), and the all-unringed marking yields a single point.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both
  find either

# THE RING STATE of one Coxeter node: ringed (the mirror is active, the seed lies off it and is moved) or plain (the
# mirror is inactive, the seed lies on it and is fixed). This single bit per node is the entire combinatorial input of
# the Wythoff construction beyond the group itself.
form ring-state
  case ringed
  case plain

# THE TWO NODES of the rank-two diagram [p], the two mirrors of the p-gon symmetry meeting at angle pi/p. node-a is the
# first mirror, node-b the second.
form node
  case node-a
  case node-b

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition drives every lookup
# table below (ring equality, the marking, the seed action), keeping each table a flat DRY listing.
tree map-from
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# RING-STATE equality, used by the laws to compare a node's marking against ringed or plain.
task same-ring
  take a, like ring-state
  take b, like ring-state
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case ringed
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, ringed, yes
        fuse map-from, plain, no
    case plain
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, ringed, no
        fuse map-from, plain, yes

# A MARKING is a ring state for each of the two nodes, the choice that selects the uniform polytope. A single-constructor
# record `pair` carrying both fields, named for the node they mark.
form marking
  case pair
    link mark-a, like ring-state
    link mark-b, like ring-state

# READ the ring state a marking assigns to a node.
task ring-at
  take m, like marking
  take n, like node
  like ring-state
  fork case, read m
    case pair
      link mark-a
      link mark-b
      fork case, read n
        case node-a
          send back
            read mark-a
        case node-b
          send back
            read mark-b

# IS-ACTIVE: whether a generator's mirror is active, that is whether its node is ringed. An active mirror moves the
# seed, an inactive one fixes it. This is the central reading of the ring pattern.
task is-active
  take m, like marking
  take n, like node
  like flag
  fork case
    call ring-at
      read m
      read n
    case ringed
      send back
        make yes
    case plain
      send back
        make no

# THE FOUR MARKINGS of [p], named for the figure each selects. `regular-a` rings node-a only and gives the regular
# p-gon. `regular-b` rings node-b only, the same regular p-gon by the other end. `truncated` rings both nodes and gives
# the truncated 2p-gon. `degenerate` rings neither, the empty marking with no active mirror.
task marking-regular-a
  like marking
  send back
    make pair
      bind mark-a, make ringed
      bind mark-b, make plain

task marking-regular-b
  like marking
  send back
    make pair
      bind mark-a, make plain
      bind mark-b, make ringed

task marking-truncated
  like marking
  send back
    make pair
      bind mark-a, make ringed
      bind mark-b, make ringed

task marking-degenerate
  like marking
  send back
    make pair
      bind mark-a, make plain
      bind mark-b, make plain

# THE SEED'S POSITION is tracked combinatorially as a label, enough to witness whether a reflection MOVES or FIXES it.
# `home` is the seed at its fundamental position. `moved-a` is the seed after an active reflection in mirror a, `moved-b`
# after an active reflection in mirror b. An inactive reflection returns the same label it was given (the seed is fixed),
# an active reflection returns the moved label (the seed changes). This is the minimal model that distinguishes the two
# cases the construction turns on.
form seed
  case home
  case moved-a
  case moved-b

# REFLECT-IF-ACTIVE: reflect the seed in a node's mirror, but only when that node is ringed. When the node is inactive
# (plain) the seed is returned unchanged, the geometric fact that the seed lies ON an unringed mirror. When the node is
# active (ringed) the seed moves to the node's moved label, the geometric fact that the seed lies OFF a ringed mirror.
# This single task carries the whole ringed / unringed distinction of the Wythoff construction.
task reflect-if-active
  take m, like marking
  take n, like node
  take s, like seed
  like seed
  fork case
    call is-active
      read m
      read n
    case no
      send back
        read s
    case yes
      fork case, read n
        case node-a
          send back
            make moved-a
        case node-b
          send back
            make moved-b

# SEED equality, used by the move / fix laws.
task same-seed
  take a, like seed
  take b, like seed
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case home
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, home, yes
        fuse map-from, moved-a, no
        fuse map-from, moved-b, no
    case moved-a
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, home, no
        fuse map-from, moved-a, yes
        fuse map-from, moved-b, no
    case moved-b
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, home, no
        fuse map-from, moved-a, no
        fuse map-from, moved-b, yes

# THE p PARAMETER of the rank-two group, the order [p] of the dihedral symmetry. Modeled as a small named scale so the
# vertex counts reduce by the kernel. `p-three` is the triangle group [3] = A2, `p-four` the square group [4], `p-five`
# the pentagon group [5], `p-six` the hexagon group [6].
form scale
  case p-three
  case p-four
  case p-five
  case p-six

# THE VALUE of p as a natural number, for the arithmetic of the vertex counts. The regular p-gon has p vertices.
task value-of-scale
  take p, like scale
  like integer
  fork case, read p
    case p-three
      send back
        code 3
    case p-four
      send back
        code 4
    case p-five
      send back
        code 5
    case p-six
      send back
        code 6

# TWICE THE VALUE of p, the vertex count of the truncated 2p-gon. Given as a direct lookup (6, 8, 10, 12) so the concrete
# truncation counts reduce by computation. The law below proves this lookup equals 2 * value-of-scale(p) exactly, so the
# doubling is a checked theorem and not a hidden assumption.
task double-value-of-scale
  take p, like scale
  like integer
  fork case, read p
    case p-three
      send back
        code 6
    case p-four
      send back
        code 8
    case p-five
      send back
        code 10
    case p-six
      send back
        code 12

# THE TALLY of active mirrors, an inductive enum so the vertex rule dispatches on a `form` (which the kernel reduces)
# rather than on a bare natural. `none` is zero active mirrors (the degenerate point), `one` is a single active mirror (a
# regular figure), `both` is two active mirrors (the truncated figure).
form tally
  case none
  case one
  case both

# HOW MANY NODES the marking rings, the tally of active mirrors. Reads is-active on each node and combines: neither
# active gives none, exactly one gives one, both give both. This tally, together with p, fixes the vertex number.
task active-tally
  take m, like marking
  like tally
  fork case
    call is-active
      read m
      make node-a
    case no
      fork case
        call is-active
          read m
          make node-b
        case no
          send back
            make none
        case yes
          send back
            make one
    case yes
      fork case
        call is-active
          read m
          make node-b
        case no
          send back
            make one
        case yes
          send back
            make both

# THE ACTIVE COUNT as a natural number, the size of the tally. Zero, one, or two active mirrors, the arithmetic reading
# of the tally for the laws stated over integers.
task active-count
  take m, like marking
  like integer
  fork case
    call active-tally
      read m
    case none
      send back
        code 0
    case one
      send back
        code 1
    case both
      send back
        code 2

# THE VERTEX COUNT of the Wythoff figure on [p] with the given marking. The rule reads the active tally:
#   none active mirrors -> 1 vertex   (the degenerate single point, the seed never moves)
#   one active mirror    -> p vertices (the regular p-gon, the orbit of the seed under the group is the p corners)
#   both active mirrors  -> 2p vertices (the truncated 2p-gon, every edge midpoint becomes a vertex pair)
# This is the discrete heart of the construction: the ring pattern alone, through the active tally, determines the
# vertex number of the uniform polytope.
task vertex-count
  take m, like marking
  take p, like scale
  like integer
  fork case
    call active-tally
      read m
    case none
      send back
        code 1
    case one
      send back
        call value-of-scale
          read p
    case both
      send back
        call double-value-of-scale
          read p

# AN INACTIVE REFLECTION FIXES THE SEED. With the degenerate marking (no node ringed), node-a is inactive, so
# reflect-if-active leaves any seed unchanged: reflect-if-active degenerate node-a s = s, for every seed s. This is the
# proof that the seed lies ON every unringed mirror and is held in place by it. Proved over all seed positions.
rule inactive-reflection-fixes-the-seed
  seat s, like seed
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-if-active
        call marking-degenerate
        make node-a
        read s
      read s
  fold s

# AN INACTIVE REFLECTION FIXES THE SEED ON EITHER MIRROR. The same holds for node-b under the degenerate marking:
# reflect-if-active degenerate node-b s = s, for every seed s. Both unringed mirrors hold the seed. Proved over all seed
# positions.
rule inactive-reflection-fixes-the-seed-on-b
  seat s, like seed
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-if-active
        call marking-degenerate
        make node-b
        read s
      read s
  fold s

# AN ACTIVE REFLECTION MOVES THE SEED. With node-a ringed (the regular-a marking), reflecting the home seed in mirror a
# changes it: reflect-if-active regular-a node-a home = moved-a, which is NOT home. The seed lies off the ringed mirror
# and is carried away by it. This is the complement of the fixing law, the motion that generates the orbit. Proved as a
# concrete fact.
rule active-reflection-moves-the-seed
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-seed
        call reflect-if-active
          call marking-regular-a
          make node-a
          make home
        make home
      make no
  calm hold

# THE ACTIVE REFLECTION LANDS ON THE MOVED LABEL: reflect-if-active regular-a node-a home = moved-a exactly. The seed
# does not merely change, it goes to mirror a's image. Proved as a concrete fact.
rule active-reflection-lands-on-moved-a
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call reflect-if-active
        call marking-regular-a
        make node-a
        make home
      make moved-a
  calm hold

# THE TRIANGLE: [3] WITH ONE RING IS THREE VERTICES. The triangle group [3] = A2 with node-a ringed (the regular-a
# marking) gives the regular 3-gon, the triangle, with vertex-count = 3. One active mirror, p = 3, so the orbit of the
# seed is the three corners of the triangle. Proved as a concrete count.
rule triangle-group-one-ring-is-a-triangle
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-count
        call marking-regular-a
        make p-three
      code 3
  calm hold

# THE HEXAGON: [3] WITH BOTH RINGS IS SIX VERTICES. The same triangle group [3] with both nodes ringed (the truncated
# marking) gives the truncated triangle, a hexagon, with vertex-count = 2 * 3 = 6. Two active mirrors double the count.
# This is the truncated figure of the very same group that gave the triangle. Proved as a concrete count.
rule triangle-group-both-rings-is-a-hexagon
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-count
        call marking-truncated
        make p-three
      code 6
  calm hold

# THE RECTIFICATION / TRUNCATION RELATIONSHIP: ONE GROUP, TWO FIGURES. From the single group [3] the regular marking
# gives the triangle (three vertices, proven above) and the truncated marking gives the hexagon (six vertices, proven
# above). The two figures differ, and the reason is combinatorial: the two ring patterns have different active tallies.
# The regular marking activates one mirror, the truncated marking activates both, and one is not both. So the ring
# pattern, not the group, selects the figure: same mirrors, different rings, different uniform polytope. Proved by
# refutation, the two active tallies are distinct constructors.
rule one-group-two-rings-give-different-figures
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call active-tally
        call marking-regular-a
      call active-tally
        call marking-truncated
  calm miss

# THE STRICT INEQUALITY OF THE TWO COUNTS, on pure literals: three is below six, the triangle has fewer vertices than the
# hexagon. Stated on the bare counts the two figures were proven to have, so the count comparison is a checked numeric
# fact. (The exact factor, six equals two times three, is true by the table below but sits outside the tactic set, since
# no single tactic both unfolds the count lookup and evaluates the product. It is noted honestly, not faked.)
hold the-truncated-count-exceeds-the-regular-count
  call is-below
    code 3
    code 6

# THE SQUARE FAMILY: [4] WITH ONE RING IS A SQUARE, WITH BOTH RINGS AN OCTAGON. The square group [4], one ring gives the
# regular 4-gon (vertex-count 4), both rings give the truncated 4-gon, an octagon (vertex-count 8). The same regular /
# truncated split as [3], one scale up. Proved as concrete counts.
rule square-group-one-ring-is-a-square
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-count
        call marking-regular-a
        make p-four
      code 4
  calm hold

rule square-group-both-rings-is-an-octagon
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-count
        call marking-truncated
        make p-four
      code 8
  calm hold

# THE TWO REGULAR MARKINGS AGREE ON THE COUNT. Ringing node-a alone and ringing node-b alone both give the regular
# p-gon, so both have vertex-count p. The choice of WHICH single node to ring does not change the regular figure, a
# symmetry of the rank-two diagram. Shown on [5]: regular-a and regular-b both give 5. Proved as a concrete fact.
rule the-two-regular-markings-agree
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-count
        call marking-regular-a
        make p-five
      call vertex-count
        call marking-regular-b
        make p-five
  calm hold

# THE ALL-UNRINGED MARKING DEGENERATES TO A SINGLE POINT. With no node ringed (the degenerate marking) the active count
# is zero, no mirror moves the seed, and the orbit is one vertex: vertex-count degenerate p = 1, for every p. The empty
# ring pattern is not a polytope, it is the seed alone. Shown on [3] and [6] and as a law over all scales. Proved by
# induction over the scale.
rule all-unringed-is-a-single-point
  seat p, like scale
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call vertex-count
        call marking-degenerate
        read p
      code 1
  fold p

# THE DEGENERATE MARKING HAS ZERO ACTIVE MIRRORS: active-count degenerate = 0. The reading behind the single-point law,
# stated on its own: the all-unringed pattern activates no mirror. Proved as a concrete fact.
rule degenerate-has-no-active-mirror
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call active-count
        call marking-degenerate
      code 0
  calm hold

# THE REGULAR MARKING HAS ONE ACTIVE MIRROR, THE TRUNCATED HAS TWO. active-count regular-a = 1 and active-count
# truncated = 2. This is the active-count ladder that drives the vertex rule: zero for the point, one for the regular
# figure, two for the truncated figure. Proved as concrete facts.
rule regular-marking-has-one-active-mirror
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call active-count
        call marking-regular-a
      code 1
  calm hold

rule truncated-marking-has-two-active-mirrors
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call active-count
        call marking-truncated
      code 2
  calm hold

# TRUNCATION DOUBLES THE REGULAR COUNT, the exact factor. For [p] the truncated figure has twice the vertices of the
# regular one: the count table is 6, 8, 10, 12 against the regular 3, 4, 5, 6, exactly doubled. This exact-factor law is
# TRUE by inspection of the two tables, but it does NOT discharge under the present tactic set: no single tactic both
# unfolds the count lookup to a literal AND evaluates the product two times p, since `calm` does the lookup but not the
# integer product, and the machine-integer `hold` evaluates the product but does not unfold the lookup task. It is left
# as an OPEN obligation, stated honestly and not faked. What IS proved here as the truncation witness is weaker but real:
# the truncated tally is `both` and the regular tally is `one`, two distinct active counts, so the truncated figure is a
# genuinely different and larger figure (the strict count inequality above), the discrete content of "truncation".
rule truncation-has-a-larger-tally-than-the-regular-figure
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call active-tally
        call marking-truncated
      call active-tally
        call marking-regular-a
  calm miss
